p.src:
Newtonian gravity writes $m\frac{d^2\bm{x}}{dt^2}
  =
  -m\bm{\nabla}\Phi,$ so the mass cancels. In the weak, slowly varying field limit, $g_{00}
  \simeq
  1+\frac{2\Phi}{c^2},
  \qquad
  \Gamma^i{}_{00}
  \simeq
  \frac{1}{c^2}\partial_i\Phi,$ and the spatial part of Eq.  reduces to $\frac{d^2x^i}{dt^2}
  \simeq
  -\partial_i\Phi.$ This is only a preview of the derivation, but it makes the destination clear: what Newtonian language calls gravitational force is encoded in the spacetime metric and its connection. A freely falling particle feels no nongravitational proper force; its worldline is straight in the geometric sense expressed by the geodesic equation.

